  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Later that night in their bedroom, Kostya braces himself for the worst and interrupts Nastya with a dramatic speech about love, forgiveness, and washing his own socks.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "У тебя сегодня всё хорошо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня да, всё хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, Настя, послушай меня сначала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Выслушай меня, пожалуйста, до конца.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Люди в жизни иногда ошибаются.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я сам перед тобой виноват.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Любить — это большое умение прощать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Понимаешь, я готов это сделать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Потому что я очень люблю тебя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но я обязательно скоро исправлюсь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я готов сам стирать носки.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Больше не буду есть на кровати.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "У тебя сегодня всё хорошо?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is everything fine with you today?' Nastya anchors the line on 'at', while 'well' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня да, всё хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'With me yes, everything is fine.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'at' and finishing on 'well'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Подожди, Настя, послушай меня сначала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Wait, Nastya, listen to me first.' Notice how Russian expresses 'wait' and 'at first' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Выслушай меня, пожалуйста, до конца.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hear me out, please, to the end.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'to listen to' before landing on 'end'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Люди в жизни иногда ошибаются.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'People sometimes make mistakes in life.' Notice how Russian expresses 'people' and 'to make a mistake' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я сам перед тобой виноват.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I myself am at fault before you.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'I' before landing on 'at fault'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Любить — это большое умение прощать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'To love is a great ability to forgive.' The grammar packs 'love' and 'to forgive' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Понимаешь, я готов это сделать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You see, I am ready to do that.' The meaning hinges on 'to understand' working together with 'to do' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Потому что я очень люблю тебя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Because I love you very much.' Kostya brings out the contrast between 'that is why' and 'what' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Но я обязательно скоро исправлюсь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But I will definitely improve soon.' In Russian, the idea of 'but' flows naturally into 'will improve' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я готов сам стирать носки.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am ready to wash my socks myself.' Notice how Russian expresses 'I' and 'socks' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Больше не буду есть на кровати.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will never eat in bed anymore.' Kostya anchors the line on 'more', while 'bed' completes the thought. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
